From: Ray Liu <ray.xy.liu@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 13 May 2026 23:50:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: AN7563 eth soc-data, init bisect, PPE flush_sram

Test boot of patch v2 (8b66d1aef0) on the AN7563 RFB:
  AN7563 dbg: ppe_init SKIPPED  <--- new default
  ... all later phases pass ...
  AN7563 dbg: register_gdm_devices done
  -> BusyBox shell prompt

So airoha_ppe_init() is the silent slab-corruption source on AArch32
AN7563. Narrow down inside ppe_init():

  - Add dev_info traces at every alloc step
    (foe / foe_flow / foe_stats / foe_check_time)
  - Add a second module parameter airoha_ppe_skip_flush_sram
    (default: 1 = skipped) wrapping the airoha_ppe_flush_sram_entries()
    call inside airoha_ppe_init().
  - Flip airoha_skip_ppe_init default back to 0 so PPE init is run
    again; if the trouble stays inside the flush_sram path, the
    board will still reach a shell because of the new skip default.

Expected outcomes for the next test boot:

  - If the board reaches a shell with airoha_ppe_skip_flush_sram=1
    and all "ppe foe alloc / foe_flow alloc / ..." printed, the
    flush_sram_entries() loop is the culprit (most likely a
    memset()/airoha_ppe_foe_commit_sram_entry() interaction that
    walks past the foe buffer on AArch32).

  - If the board panics with airoha_ppe_skip_flush_sram=1 and the
    last printed dbg is one of the foe alloc lines, then a
    dmam_alloc_coherent() inside ppe_init() is the source (then we
    will reduce foe table size or move to plain DMA pool).

Signed-off-by: Ray Liu <ray.xy.liu@gmail.com>
---
